  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Patchy fog and low stratus less likely tonight in western \n  Montana due to developing easterly winds.\n\n- Unsettled weather pattern next week with multiple systems \n  capable of mountain and mountain pass snow.\n\nAreas of fog and low stratus have been slow to erode today, \nparticularly for the valleys of northwest and west-central \nMontana. Satellite imagery reveals gradual eroding occurring from\neast to west, with most of this low level cloud cover expected to\nbe dissipated by late afternoon. Western Montana and north \ncentral Idaho will remain under a weak area of high pressure \nthrough Sunday night, bringing generally benign weather \nconditions. \n\nAn easterly wind gradient sets up across the region tonight, which\nshould allow for light winds to persist into the overnight and\nearly morning hours. This should keep additional fog and low\nstratus from forming once again, especially across western\nMontana. These winds will also likely keep temperatures from \nreaching as cold as previous forecasts indicated. The east winds \nare anticipated to strengthen through the day Sunday, likely \npeaking Sunday evening with localized areas seeing gusts 25 to 35 \nmph, including over the eastern side of Flathead Lake. \n\nOn Monday, a low pressure system is expected to dive into the\nPacific northwest, tracking through central Idaho and western\nMontana Monday night into Tuesday. This will allow for another\nround of precipitation to develop Monday night, generally along\nand south of Interstate 90, then spreading northwards into\nnorthwest Montana Tuesday afternoon into Wednesday morning.\nForecast models indicate snow levels will lower to between 5000 to\n6000 feet, which will allow for accumulating snow in the higher \nterrain and may bring periods of slick travel over mountain \npasses, especially in the overnight and early morning hours.\n\nForecast models are coming into better agreement on another,\npotentially colder, low pressure system moving over the region\nThursday into the weekend. While the overall details remain murky,\nthere is potential for snow level to lower further, especially in\nnorthwest Montana and along the COntinental Divide, where its not\nout of the question to see periods of snow even into valley\nlocations.",
